---
title: "Upgrade to v1.11"
description: "Breaking changes, migration steps and behavior changes when upgrading Outpost from v1.10 to v1.11."
---

This guide covers what changes when upgrading from v1.10 to v1.11. There are two breaking changes and one Redis migration to review before upgrading.

Run `outpost migrate apply --yes` before starting v1.11: the server refuses to start while a migration is pending. There is no new PostgreSQL or ClickHouse migration. Retries scheduled by v1.10 run on v1.11.

| Change | Impact | Action Required |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Custom header updates merge per header](#custom-header-updates-merge-per-header) | Clients that update a webhook destination's `config.custom_headers` | Remove headers with `null` instead of leaving them out |
| [Tenant JWTs need `API_JWT_SECRET`](#tenant-jwts-need-api_jwt_secret) | Deployments that use the tenant portal or tenant JWTs without `API_JWT_SECRET` | Set `API_JWT_SECRET` |
| [IDs are escaped in Redis keys](#ids-are-escaped-in-redis-keys) | Tenant or destination IDs that contain `%`, `:`, `{` or `}` | Check `outpost migrate plan` and pick an upgrade order |

For new features and fixes, see the [v1.11.0 release notes](https://github.com/hookdeck/outpost/releases/tag/v1.11.0).

## Breaking Changes

### Custom header updates merge per header

A `PATCH` to a webhook destination's `config.custom_headers` now updates the headers you send and keeps the others. v1.10 replaced the whole object.

| `custom_headers` in the update | v1.10 | v1.11 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A header left out | removed | kept |
| A header set to `null` | rejected | removed |
| `{}` | all headers removed | no change |
| The field set to `null` | all headers removed | all headers removed |

If your client removes a header by sending the object without it, send that header as `null` instead. Creating a destination is unchanged. The new `credentials.custom_headers` field follows the same rules. See [Custom Headers](/docs/outpost/destinations/webhook#custom-headers).

### Tenant JWTs need `API_JWT_SECRET`

Tenant JWTs and the tenant portal now require `API_JWT_SECRET`. Without it, a tenant JWT gets a `401`, and `GET /tenants/{tenant_id}/token` and `GET /tenants/{tenant_id}/portal` return `501`. v1.10 signed and accepted tenant JWTs with an empty key when the secret was unset. The API key works as before.

If you use the tenant portal or tenant JWTs, set `API_JWT_SECRET` before upgrading. Tokens issued by v1.10 with the secret set keep working. See the [configuration reference](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#required-variables).

## Migration

### IDs are escaped in Redis keys

Tenant, destination and event IDs are now percent-encoded where they appear in Redis keys, so IDs that contain `%`, `:`, `{` or `}` can no longer share a key with another tenant's. The Redis migration `004_escape_ids` moves the stored tenants and destinations whose IDs contain those characters. Other keys are unchanged.

Run `outpost migrate plan` with the v1.11 image to see how many keys move:

```
Redis Migrations (1 pending):
  - redis/004_escape_ids: Escape tenant and destination IDs in tenant store keys
      keys_to_move: 0
```

With `keys_to_move: 0`, run `outpost migrate apply --yes` and upgrade as usual.

Otherwise, v1.10 no longer finds the moved tenants and destinations once the migration has run. Until every v1.10 instance has stopped, API requests for them through a v1.10 instance return `404`, and events published for them through it are accepted but not delivered. Pick one:

- **Stop v1.10 first:** stop every instance, run `outpost migrate apply --yes`, then start v1.11. Nothing is lost; the API is unavailable in between.
- **Rolling update:** run `outpost migrate apply --yes`, then replace the instances. Until the last v1.10 instance stops, don't create or update the affected tenants or their destinations, and re-publish events sent for them in that window.

The migration can be run again. If it stops on an error, it stays pending and the next run continues where it stopped. See the [Schema Migration Guide](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/guides/migration).

## Behavior Changes

- Publish idempotency is per tenant. The same event `id` published for two tenants is delivered to both; v1.10 marked the second one as a duplicate for `PUBLISH_IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_TTL`. See [Event Structure](/docs/outpost/publishing/events#event-structure).
- Around the upgrade, an event or attempt can be delivered one extra time: idempotency keys written by v1.10 aren't recognized by v1.11, and a retry scheduled by v1.10 isn't cancelled by a manual retry on v1.11. For IDs that contain `%`, `:`, `{` or `}`, the consecutive failure count and the exhausted-retries alert window also start over once.
- `config` and `credentials` keys that a destination type doesn't define are ignored on create and update, and are no longer returned. Keys already stored are removed on the destination's next update.
- `aws_sqs` destinations mask `credentials.session` in API responses, as the other AWS destinations do.

## Rolling Back

Rolling back to v1.10.0 doesn't require reverting the migration, with two limits:

- Tenants and destinations moved by `004_escape_ids` are not found by v1.10.
- v1.10 doesn't send `credentials.custom_headers`, and returns their values unmasked in API responses.

## New Configuration

- `PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_ROLE_ARN`, `PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_EXTERNAL_ID`, `PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_ROLE_SESSION_NAME` and `PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_STS_REGION`: read the SQS publish queue through an assumed IAM role, for example a queue in another AWS account. See [Publish from SQS](/docs/outpost/publishing/publish-from-sqs).
